w(['<li><b>Tension + shear (17.8.3).</b> The same anchors pull T. Tension ratio: the largest of bar steel, pullout, blowout and the anchor reinforcement. For the reinforcement it is the steel of every beam bar still developed inside the body (CY: %d bars, %.2f). ' ...
   'Both ratios are over 0.2, so N/&phi;N<sub>n</sub> + V/&phi;V<sub>n</sub> &le; 1.2. CY: (%.2f + %.2f)/1.2 = %.2f. Worst type: %s, (%.2f + %.2f)/1.2 = <b>%.2f</b>.</li>'], ...
   R.cone(j4).kTi, St.nT(j4), St.nT(j4), St.s(1,j4), St.int(1,j4), R.typ(jw).name, St.nT(jw), St.s(1,jw), St.int(1,jw));
w(['<li><b>Reading.</b> The top of the pedestal cuts the half cone (CY: %g mm above the anchors, &psi;<sub>ed,V</sub> = %.2f, A<sub>Vc</sub> cut), but H is small: the torsion couple has a %.0f mm lever arm down to the shear anchors. ' ...
   'Not counted: &psi;<sub>c,V</sub> = 1.2 for the &#216;16 column bars that run along the vertical edge between the anchors and the side face (Table 17.7.2.5.1), and the beams that frame into some side faces.</li>'], ...
   St.ctop(j4), St.pedV(2,j4), Y4.zT - S.zS);
w(['<li><b>If all four holes bear.</b> With V/4 on each top anchor the interaction reaches %.2f (E) and %.2f (CY): over 1 at E, so the concrete beside the top anchors could crack sideways. ' ...
   'Their share of V then moves to the shear anchors, which carry all of V on their own (section 8, worst %.2f). This is the same idea as case 2 of R17.7.2.1: a near anchor gives up its share and the others take it, because the holes are not welded. H stays on the top anchors, and that is the check above. ' ...
   'To make this certain rather than argued, the holes of the top anchors in the end plate can be slotted vertically, about 18 x 26 mm with a plate washer. The top anchors then never pick up V, but they still take H.</li>'], St.int(2,1), St.int(2,j4), max(sqrt(S.ell2)));
